.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn channel_wait
returns CHANNEL_WAIT_READ and/or CHANNEL_WAIT_WRITE readiness bits, zero on
timeout, or minus one with errno on failure.
A negative timeout waits indefinitely; zero polls once without sleeping.
Positive timeouts use one monotonic deadline across interrupted receives,
kqueue waits, and compatibility fallback.
Signals and wakeups do not restart the original timeout budget.
Scheduler delays can still postpone when the calling thread runs again.
.Pp
The
.Nm
library provides a nonblocking, event-loop-native abstraction over
.Dv MAC_CAPABILITY_SENDMSG
and
.Dv MAC_CAPABILITY_RECVMSG ,
and a synchronous request through
.Dv MAC_CAPABILITY_CHANNEL_CALL
.Pq see Sx SYNCHRONOUS CALLS .
It does not use or expose
.Dv MAC_CAPABILITY_CALL .
.Pp
A channel owns the descriptor passed successfully to
.Fn channel_create .
The descriptor is made nonblocking and is closed by
.Fn channel_destroy .
The caller registers
.Fn channel_fd
with kqueue for read readiness, and for write readiness while messages are
queued.
.Fn channel_dispatch
drains received messages and
.Fn channel_flush
drains the bounded outgoing queue.
.Fn channel_wants_write
reports whether write readiness should be enabled.
.Pp
Requests have fresh nonzero correlation tokens.
Events have token zero.
Replies are matched to outstanding requests before provider-side request
classification.
Unknown or stale replies are discarded and all of their descriptors are
closed.
Peer death completes every outstanding request with an error.
.Pp
Roles are deliberately directional.
A client may originate requests and a provider receives nonzero-token
requests and responds.
Either role may send events.
A provider cannot originate a request because an unknown late reply would
otherwise be indistinguishable from a new request without adding a redundant
transport envelope.
.Pp
Payloads and descriptors passed for sending remain caller-owned.
While nothing is queued, a send hands the message to the kernel directly
from the caller's memory in one system call, without allocating; messages
are queued only once the kernel has refused one, and from then on behind the
waiting ones, so delivery order is that of the sends.
When backpressure requires queuing,
.Nm
copies the payload and duplicates the descriptors into one block per
message.
.Fn channel_get_stats
counts both outcomes; see
.Sx STATISTICS .
The
.Sx SEND BUFFERS
interface lets a caller encode straight into the memory the kernel reads.
Ownership of a received message is transferred to the installed handler.
The handler may retain a provider request and respond later.
Received messages own their installed descriptors.
.Fn channel_message_take_fd
transfers one descriptor to the caller; all untaken descriptors are closed
when the message is freed.
Every delivered message must eventually be passed to
.Fn channel_message_free .
Each message is one allocation sized from recent traffic on its channel,
not the maximum message size, so a retained request costs about its own
payload plus a fixed header.
A channel keeps the most recently freed message block (up to 4 KiB, or
larger while the traffic needs it), one released outgoing block of the same
size class, and released requests up to its pending-request limit, for
reuse, so a steady request/reply exchange, however deeply pipelined, does not
allocate per call once warm; all are freed with the channel.
.Pp
Handlers execute synchronously from
.Fn channel_dispatch .
The API is intended for one dispatch context and does not create threads.
A handler may request destruction; actual storage reclamation is deferred
until dispatch unwinds.
.Pp
.Fn channel_abandon
is the post-fork escape hatch.
It releases inherited heap bookkeeping without invoking callbacks or closing
numeric descriptor slots which may already have been closed and reused in the
child.
It must not be used for ordinary shutdown.
.Pp
Cancellation is local only.
A late remote response is treated as stale and discarded.
Remote cancellation, application schemas, operation codes, delivery
guarantees, and descriptor-slot meanings belong to the service protocol
above this library.
.Sh CHANNEL CREATION
The caller initializes
.Vt struct channel_options
with
.Dv CHANNEL_OPTIONS_INITIALIZER .
The
.Va size
field must be at least the structure size.
Larger structures are accepted for forward-compatible extension.
The role is one of:
.Bl -tag -width CHANNEL_ROLE_PROVIDER
.It Dv CHANNEL_ROLE_CLIENT
May originate requests.
An unmatched nonzero token is a stale reply and is discarded.
.It Dv CHANNEL_ROLE_PROVIDER
May receive nonzero-token requests and respond to them.
It cannot originate requests.
.El
.Pp
The remaining fields bound pending requests and locally queued messages,
payload bytes, and descriptors.
All limits must be selected before untrusted traffic is accepted.
A successful
.Fn channel_create
consumes
.Fa fd .
The channel uses a private close-on-fork and close-on-exec duplicate, so its
descriptor number is not required to equal
.Fa fd ;
call
.Fn channel_fd
for event-loop registration.
On failure the caller retains it.
.Pp
Every send accepts a versioned
.Vt struct channel_outgoing .
Its
.Va data
and
.Va fds
members remain borrowed for the duration of the call.
The payload must be nonempty, and attachment slots are interpreted only by
the service protocol above
.Nm .
The
.Va size
field is either
.Dv CHANNEL_OUTGOING_SIZE_V1 ,
for callers built before
.Va fd_specs
and
.Va flags
existed, which are then not read, or at least the current structure size.
.Sh DESCRIPTOR ATTACHMENTS
Every send hands the message and all of its descriptors to the kernel in one
system call, however many descriptors are attached.
Optional per-descriptor work is folded into that call:
.Bl -tag -width CHANNEL_SEND_LOCK_INHERIT
.It Va fd_specs
Null, or one
.Vt struct channel_fd_spec
per descriptor.
.Va type
is
.Dv CHANNEL_FD_TYPE_ANY ,
.Dv CHANNEL_FD_TYPE_SOCKET ,
or
.Dv CHANNEL_FD_TYPE_CHANNEL .
.Va rights
is zero, which keeps the descriptor's rights, or a combination of
.Dv CHANNEL_FD_RIGHT_READ
and
.Dv CHANNEL_FD_RIGHT_WRITE ;
socket expectations also require
.Dv CAP_FSTAT .
Every expectation is checked before the message is accepted; a mismatch fails
the whole send with
.Er EFTYPE ,
.Er ENOTCAPABLE ,
or
.Er EOPNOTSUPP ,
and consumes no transfer authority.
Requested rights narrow only the delivered copy, as
.Xr cap_rights_limit 2
would, and never the caller's descriptor.
.Dv CHANNEL_FD_LOCK_INHERIT
in
.Va flags
delivers that descriptor with close-on-exec and close-on-fork locked.
.It Dv CHANNEL_SEND_MOVE
Transfers ownership of the attached descriptors once the send is accepted,
whether it was sent at once or queued behind backpressure; they must then not
be used or closed by the caller.
A queued message adopts them without duplicating them.
After a failed send the caller still owns them, unchanged.
.It Dv CHANNEL_SEND_LOCK_INHERIT
Applies
.Dv CHANNEL_FD_LOCK_INHERIT
to every attached descriptor.
.El
.Pp
On kernels without this support,
.Nm
performs the same checks itself, narrowing or locking a private duplicate,
with equivalent results at the cost of additional system calls.
.Pp
.Fn channel_message_fd_info
reports the kernel's view of an attached descriptor, recorded when the message
was received: its type
.Pq Dv CHANNEL_FD_TYPE_OTHER No when neither socket nor channel
and its rights as
.Dv CHANNEL_FD_RIGHT_READ ,
.Dv CHANNEL_FD_RIGHT_WRITE ,
and
.Dv CHANNEL_FD_RIGHT_FSTAT ,
with
.Dv CHANNEL_FD_RIGHTS_EXACT
when it holds no other rights and
.Dv CHANNEL_FD_RIGHTS_ALL
when it is unrestricted.
A receiver can validate an attachment from this report without further system
calls.
It fails with
.Er ENOTSUP
when the kernel does not report descriptors.
.Sh EVENT LOOP
.Fn channel_fd
returns the borrowed kernel descriptor.
Register it for
.Dv EVFILT_READ .
After a send operation, call
.Fn channel_wants_write ;
enable
.Dv EVFILT_WRITE
only while it returns one.
.Fn channel_dispatch
processes at most 64 messages per call so one busy channel cannot monopolize
an event loop.
It returns the number processed, including messages discarded by policy.
.Fn channel_flush
attempts to drain the outgoing queue and treats continued
.Er EAGAIN
as successful retained backpressure.
.Pp
The library has no internal lock.
Creation, dispatch, sending, cancellation, and destruction of one channel must
be serialized by its owner.
Callbacks run synchronously in the thread calling
.Fn channel_dispatch .
They may retain messages, enqueue responses or events, cancel other requests,
or call
.Fn channel_destroy .
They must not recursively dispatch the same channel.
.Sh REQUESTS AND RESPONSES
.Fn channel_send_request
registers
.Fa handler
before attempting the send, generates a fresh nonzero token, and returns a
request handle through
.Fa request .
The payload must be nonempty.
The input payload and descriptor array remain caller-owned.
The handler is invoked exactly once with either a reply and error zero, or a
null reply and a terminal or cancellation error.
The handler owns a nonnull reply and must free it.
.Pp
.Fn channel_send_reply
uses the token and channel retained by an incoming provider request.
It rejects token-zero messages, client-side replies, and duplicate responses.
A provider may keep the request after its handler returns and respond
asynchronously.
It must eventually free the request even if no response is sent.
.Pp
.Fn channel_request_cancel
removes an unsent queued request when possible and completes its callback with
.Er ECANCELED .
Cancellation does not send a remote message.
A response already in flight is later recognized as stale.
.Fn channel_request_status
returns
.Er EINPROGRESS
while pending and otherwise the completion error, with zero denoting a reply.
.Fn channel_request_release
releases the caller's handle reference.
It may be called while the request is pending or by its callback; reclamation
is deferred until completion when necessary.
.Sh SYNCHRONOUS CALLS
A request whose reply is awaited at once costs a send, a readiness wait and
a receive system call, plus the clock reads bounding the wait.
.Fn channel_call_request
is
.Fn channel_send_request
whose reply, on kernels with
.Dv MAC_CAPABILITY_CHANNEL_CALL ,
is received in the same system call: when nothing is queued ahead of it the
calling thread sleeps in the kernel for the message carrying the request's
token, events and other replies that arrive meanwhile stay queued for
.Fn channel_dispatch ,
and
.Fa handler
runs with the reply before the function returns, so
.Fa request
may already have been passed to it.
After
.Fa timeout_ms
milliseconds
.Pq negative: no limit
the handler runs with
.Er ETIMEDOUT
and no reply; the request remains sent and its late reply is discarded,
with every attached descriptor closed, exactly as a reply to a cancelled
request is.
Peer death completes the handler with the terminal error.
When the kernel lacks the command, output is queued behind backpressure, or
the kernel refuses the request with
.Er EAGAIN ,
the request is sent or queued as by
.Fn channel_send_request
and completes from
.Fn channel_dispatch ;
.Fa timeout_ms
then does not apply, and
.Fn channel_request_status
reports
.Er EINPROGRESS .
A reply larger than the receive block, or a call interrupted by a signal,
also completes from
.Fn channel_dispatch .
The two paths are the same request as the kernel sees it: descriptor
expectations, transfer authority, moves and queue limits apply identically,
and a failed expectation fails the call before anything is sent.
While the caller sleeps in the kernel it does not drain its receive queue;
the reply itself is handed to it even when that queue is full, but a provider
that sends more events than the queue holds
.Pq the receive depth reported by Dv MAC_CAPABILITY_GETINFO
before replying is held back until the caller dispatches, which a caller
with a timeout observes as
.Er ETIMEDOUT .
Providers replying to synchronous callers should bound the events they emit
before the reply, as they must for any client that waits for one message.
.Pp
.Fn channel_call
is the form for callers without an event loop.
It issues the request, runs the wait and dispatch loop itself when the kernel
did not complete it, applying
.Fa timeout_ms
to the whole call, and returns the reply, a
.Dv CHANNEL_MESSAGE_REPLY
the caller frees.
Other messages received meanwhile reach the installed handlers as from
.Fn channel_dispatch .
It fails with
.Er ETIMEDOUT ,
leaving the channel usable, or with the terminal channel error.
.Pp
Whether the kernel path is available is decided once per channel, from the
descriptor's ioctl allowlist and the
.Dv MAC_CAPABILITY_INFO_F_CHANNEL_CALL
feature, without ever issuing an unsupported command.
Setting the environment variable
.Ev LIBCHANNEL_NO_CHANNEL_CALL
.Pq ignored by set-user-ID programs
forces the ordinary path, for comparison.
.Sh EVENTS
.Fn channel_send_event
sends a token-zero message.
Either role may send events.
.Fn channel_set_event_handler
installs the receiver.
When no event handler is installed, events are discarded and all attached
descriptors are closed.
.Fn channel_set_request_handler
installs the provider request handler.
When no request handler is installed, incoming requests are discarded.
.Sh SEND BUFFERS
A caller that produces its payload incrementally, such as a message codec,
can encode straight into the memory the kernel will read instead of into a
buffer of its own that the send then copies when it must queue.
.Fn channel_outgoing_reserve
yields a buffer with room for
.Fa max_length
payload bytes, between one and the kernel maximum, and up to
.Fa nfds
descriptors; the channel's spare block serves it when it fits, so a steady
exchange reserves and commits without allocating.
.Fn channel_outgoing_buffer_data
returns the writable payload area and
.Fn channel_outgoing_buffer_capacity
the reserved
.Fa max_length .
Nothing is sent or accounted at reservation; a reserved buffer counts toward
no queue limit.
.Pp
A commit sends the payload's first
.Fa length
bytes, between one and the reservation, with up to the reserved number of
descriptors.
.Fn channel_outgoing_commit_event ,
.Fn channel_outgoing_commit_request
and
.Fn channel_outgoing_commit_reply
take the same descriptors, expectations and
.Dv CHANNEL_SEND_*
flags as the
.Vt struct channel_outgoing
members of
.Fn channel_send_event ,
.Fn channel_send_request
and
.Fn channel_send_reply ,
have the same role and request requirements, register a request before the
message is handed to the kernel, and report the same errors.
The reply form requires the request message to belong to the buffer's
channel.
While nothing is queued ahead the message goes to the kernel from the buffer
itself; when it must queue, the buffer becomes the queue entry without
copying the payload, adopting moved descriptors or duplicating lent ones as
the ordinary sends do.
Either way a successful commit consumes the buffer: it is no longer the
caller's, and
.Dv CHANNEL_SEND_MOVE
descriptors have been transferred.
.Pp
A failed commit changes nothing: the buffer stays reserved with its payload,
the caller's descriptors remain open and the request is not registered, so
the caller may fix the arguments and commit again, or abort.
.Fn channel_outgoing_abort
releases a reserved buffer; nothing is attached to it before commit, so
abort only returns memory.
A reserved buffer references its channel the way a received message does.
It may be aborted, and a commit fails with the channel's terminal error,
after
.Fn channel_destroy ;
in a
.Xr fork 2
child it may only be aborted, which frees memory and closes nothing.
Every reserved buffer must eventually be committed or aborted.
.Sh STATISTICS
.Fn channel_get_stats
fills a
.Vt struct channel_stats
whose
.Va size
the caller sets first
.Pq Dv CHANNEL_STATS_INITIALIZER .
.Va sent_direct
counts messages the kernel accepted from the caller's memory,
.Va sent_queued
messages queued behind backpressure and
.Va sent_flushed
the queued ones since accepted by
.Fn channel_flush ,
cumulatively over the channel's lifetime.
.Va pending_requests ,
.Va queued_messages ,
.Va queued_bytes
and
.Va queued_fds
are the current gauges against the limits set at creation.
.Sh MESSAGE OWNERSHIP
The following accessors inspect a received message:
.Bl -tag -width channel_message_fd_count
.It Fn channel_message_kind
Returns request, reply, or event classification.
.It Fn channel_message_data
Returns borrowed payload storage valid until the message is freed.
.It Fn channel_message_length
Returns the kernel-reported payload length.
.It Fn channel_message_token
Returns the channel token; events return zero.
.It Fn channel_message_sender
Returns borrowed authenticated badge, nonce, uid, gid, and prison metadata.
.It Fn channel_message_process
Returns borrowed kernel-stamped process lifetime and responsible-process
identities, with diagnostic PIDs, when versioned metadata is available.
The original sender is preserved through kernel channel forwarding.
A null pointer means the kernel or descriptor rights supplied only legacy
metadata; callers must not invent attribution from an untrusted payload.
Responsible-process association is delegated attribution, not authorization
or proof that the origin approved every subsequent request.
.It Fn channel_message_fd_count
Returns the exact attachment count.
.It Fn channel_message_borrow_fd
Returns a borrowed installed descriptor for one zero-based attachment slot.
.It Fn channel_message_take_fd
Transfers one installed descriptor to the caller and marks its slot empty.
.El
.Pp
.Fn channel_message_free
closes every untaken descriptor and releases the payload and retained channel
reference.
Attachment slots preserve sender array order; sender descriptor numbers are
never part of the message.
.Sh SHUTDOWN AND FORK
.Fn channel_destroy
closes the descriptor, drops queued messages and descriptors, and completes
pending requests with
.Er ECANCELED .
When called by a callback, final reclamation waits until dispatch unwinds.
.Pp
.Fn channel_abandon
is only for the child side of
.Xr fork 2
when callbacks and locks may belong to vanished threads.
Channel descriptors and hidden queued attachment duplicates are locked
close-on-fork and close-on-exec; moved attachments keep the caller's
inheritance state while queued.
Every channel and received attachment is process-bound.
An inherited operation fails with
.Er ECHILD .
.Fn channel_abandon
then frees inherited heap bookkeeping without invoking callbacks or closing
numeric descriptor slots which may already have been closed and reused in the
child.
.Pp
.Fn channel_error
returns zero while healthy.
After peer death or another terminal transport failure it returns \-1 and
restores the stable terminal error in
.Va errno .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Unless documented otherwise, functions returning
.Vt int
return zero on success and \-1 on failure.
.Fn channel_dispatch
returns a nonnegative message count on success.
.Fn channel_fd
returns a nonnegative borrowed descriptor on success.
.Fn channel_wants_write
returns zero or one.
.Fn channel_request_status
returns an errno value directly.
.Fn channel_outgoing_buffer_data
returns a null pointer, with
.Er EINVAL ,
for a buffer that is not reserved, and
.Fn channel_outgoing_buffer_capacity
zero.
Message accessors return their documented neutral value for a null message.
.Sh ERRORS
Outgoing limits produce
.Er ENOBUFS .
A synchronous call that exceeds its timeout fails with
.Er ETIMEDOUT .
Backpressure is retained internally and is not reported as failure.
Protocol-role and ownership violations produce
.Er EINVAL .
Invalid or already completed cancellation produces
.Er EALREADY .
Payloads larger than the kernel channel maximum, unknown outgoing flags, and
an outgoing
.Va size
that is neither layout are rejected with
.Er EINVAL ,
as are a commit of zero bytes, of more bytes or descriptors than were
reserved, or of a buffer that is not reserved.
A failed allocation while reserving, queuing or registering a request
produces
.Er ENOMEM
and leaves the caller's descriptors and the channel's accounting unchanged.
Descriptor expectation failures are described in
.Sx DESCRIPTOR ATTACHMENTS ;
they do not end the channel.
The terminal channel error is available through
.Fn channel_error .
.Sh DTRACE PROVIDER
When built with DTrace support,
.Nm
publishes the
.Qq libchannel
USDT provider.
Its probes cover channel creation, immediate sends, local queueing, received
message classification, request completion, policy discards, and terminal
peer death.
Payload contents and descriptor numbers are never exposed by these probes.
.Sh FIDL INTEGRATION
The experimental FIDL source import does not change this API or its
current wire and descriptor ownership contracts.
An experimental native backend and generated C bridge now use this library.
Production services still require an explicit protocol migration.
See
.Xr fidl 7
for status and adaptation requirements.
